"executive_summary": "This study provides the first direct experimental visualization of the π-hole, a submolecular electron-deficient cavity, in 9,10-Dichlorooctafluoroanthracene (FCl-An) using Kelvin Probe Force Microscopy (KPFM). While the existence of π-holes was previously predicted by quantum calculations and inferred indirectly from intermolecular interaction data, this work confirms their presence by mapping the local contact potential difference (LCPD) with atomic resolution. The researchers compared FCl-An with unsubstituted anthracene (An) on an Au(111) surface. Results show that FCl-An exhibits a characteristic 'bow-tie' pattern in LCPD maps, corresponding to the positive electrostatic potential of the π-hole, whereas An does not. Furthermore, the study demonstrates that the π-hole significantly alters the molecule's interaction with the metallic substrate. Specifically, the π-hole increases the ionization potential of FCl-An, which suppresses charge induction at the molecule/metal interface. This leads to weaker induced electrostatic interactions and a measurable increase in the adsorption height of FCl-An compared to An. The findings validate theoretical predictions regarding the role of submolecular charge distribution in non-covalent interactions and adsorption phenomena, highlighting KPFM as a powerful tool for real-space observation of molecular electrostatics.",
